  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nCurrently, a deep trough is digging over the Great Lakes region with \na building ridge over the Central Rockies. The forecast area is \nsituated between these two features under northwesterly flow. Water \nvapor imagery depicts an extremely dry air mass overhead in the \nwake of yesterday's cold front. Current PWATs over the eastern half \nof the state sit at around 0.50 inch, just below the 25th percentile \nfor this time of year. Despite the drier air, clear skies will allow \nfor efficient radiational cooling early this morning, leading to \nareas of ground fog. Widespread and significant impacts are not \nexpected. As the region remains under the influence of the post-\nfrontal air mass through today, temperatures right around normal \nare expected areawide, reaching into the middle to upper 70s.\n\nRidging will continue to build into the region from the west, with \nthe ridge center shifting over the Desert Southwest and the Southern \nPlains over the next several days. A weak perturbation in the \npattern on Tuesday night associated with a trough digging into the \nGreat Lakes may slow the warming trend as it acts to displace the \nridge further south. But anomalous heights will continue to lead to \nafternoon temperatures reaching above normal through the end of the \nforecast period. Highs are generally expected to reach into the \nlower to middle 80s areawide for Tue-Sun. Overall, mostly clear \nskies, dry conditions, and anomalous warmth will persist through the \nend of the forecast period.",
